course: everyday
lesson: 209
title: Sharing, sleep and safe food
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Colds, flu and cold sores spread through spit, so you drink from your own cup or bottle and do not share one with a friend.
explain: The body falls asleep more easily in a dark, quiet, cool room; the light and excitement of a tablet or phone make sleep harder, so screens go away about an hour before bed.
explain: Food can be dangerous in hidden ways: dirt on unwashed fruit, a food a friend is allergic to, or a whole grape that can block a small child's airway.
explain: A girl in Melbourne cuts grapes lengthways for her little brother, because small round food can get stuck in a small child's throat.
explain: Some old rules are myths: you can swim after a normal meal, perhaps waiting a little after a very big one.
fact: You use your own cup or bottle, because colds, flu and cold sores spread through spit.
fact: When dressing, underwear goes on first, then clothes, then socks and shoes.
fact: Screens are put away about an hour before sleep and kept out of the bedroom.
fact: The light and excitement of screens make it harder to fall asleep.
fact: If you cannot fall asleep, you lie quietly, breathe slowly or read a calm book.
fact: A dark, quiet and cool bedroom helps deep sleep.
fact: Fruit and vegetables are washed before eating, as they may have dirt or chemicals on them.
fact: You share a friend's lunch only if neither of you has allergies and adults agree, because some foods are dangerous for people with allergies.
fact: A healthy snack between meals, such as fruit, yoghurt or nuts if you are not allergic, keeps your energy steady.
fact: Grapes are cut lengthways for small children, because small round food can block a small child's airway.
fact: There is no good evidence that swimming after a normal meal causes drowning; the real safety rule is to swim only where an adult is watching.
exercise: Your friend offers you a drink from her water bottle. What is best? | Use your own cup or bottle | Drink from hers, as she looks healthy | Wipe the top with your sleeve and drink | Share only on really hot days
exercise: Why should you not share a bottle? | Colds, flu and cold sores spread through spit | The bottle will empty far too fast if two people drink from it | Water tastes different from another bottle | Bottles break when two people use them
exercise: In what order do you get dressed? | Underwear, then clothes, then socks and shoes | Shoes, then socks, then clothes, then a coat on top | Clothes, then underwear on top | Socks and shoes, then underwear
exercise: You like playing games on a tablet in bed. What helps you sleep better? | Put screens away about an hour before bed and out of the bedroom | Play until you fall asleep with the tablet | Turn the brightness up so your eyes get tired | Watch an exciting film to wear you out
exercise: Why do screens at bedtime make sleep harder? | Their light and excitement keep the brain awake | They make the room too cold | They use up your body's water | They make you hungry
exercise: You have been lying awake for a while. What helps? | Lie quietly, breathe slowly or read a calm book | Get up and play a loud video game until you feel sleepy | Eat a big bowl of sweets | Do star jumps to tire yourself out
exercise: What kind of bedroom helps deep sleep? | Dark, quiet and cool | Bright, noisy and hot | Bright and warm with music on | Messy, with the TV left on
exercise: You buy apples from the shop. What do you do before eating one? | Wash it | Nothing, shop fruit is always clean | Peel off all of the skin and the core | Put it in the freezer first
exercise: Why are fruit and vegetables washed? | They may have dirt or chemicals on them | Washing them in cold water makes them taste sweeter | Washing adds vitamins | It makes them stay fresh for weeks
exercise: At school a friend offers you half her peanut sandwich. When is sharing OK? | Only if neither of you has allergies and adults agree | Always, sharing is kind | Only if the sandwich looks fresh | Only if you swap something back
exercise: You are hungry long before dinner. What is a good snack? | Fruit, yoghurt or nuts if you are not allergic | A big bag of sweets | A fizzy drink and crisps | Nothing at all until dinner
exercise: Why are grapes cut lengthways for a toddler? | Small round food can block a small child's airway | Toddlers do not like round shapes and will push them away | Cut grapes taste sweeter | It makes the grapes last longer
exercise: Your aunt says you must wait an hour after lunch before swimming. What is true? | You can swim after a normal meal, perhaps waiting a little after a huge one | You will surely drown if you swim within an hour | You must wait three hours after eating | You must never eat on swimming days
test: A boy in Dublin has a cold sore. Why should his sister not drink from his bottle? | Cold sores spread through spit | Cold sores spread only through the air | The bottle would make her cough | Cold sores are only on his skin and cannot spread
test: A girl reads a calm book in her dark, cool room instead of watching videos. Why does this help? | Calm activities and darkness help the body relax into sleep | Books make you tired because they are always boring to read | Cool rooms make you hungry | Videos only harm sleep in winter
test: Which bedroom is best for sleep? | A dark, cool room with the phone charging in the kitchen | A warm room with the TV on low, so you drift off to the sound | A bright room with the phone by the pillow | A noisy room with the light on
test: A boy eats strawberries straight from the punnet without washing them. What could be on them? | Dirt or chemicals | Extra vitamins only | Nothing, they grow clean | Only a little sugar
test: A new friend at school offers you a cake with nuts. You have a nut allergy. What do you do? | Say no thank you, as nuts are dangerous for you | Eat a small piece to be polite, then stop eating it | Pick the nuts out and eat the rest | Eat it and tell the teacher later
test: You are babysitting with Grandma and your two-year-old cousin wants grapes. What do you do? | Ask Grandma to cut them lengthways first | Give him whole grapes, as they are soft enough to chew | Give a handful so he eats fast | Give him hard sweets instead
test: A friend in Florida eats a sandwich at the pool and wants to swim straight after. What is true? | He can swim after a normal meal | He will drown if he swims within an hour | He must wait until the next day to swim | He must swim only in the shallow end forever
test: Why is fruit a better snack than sweets between meals? | It keeps your energy steady | It has no sugar at all | Sweets have no energy | Fruit makes you never hungry again
test: A boy keeps putting his shoes on before his trousers. What is the quicker order? | Underwear, clothes, then socks and shoes | Shoes first, then pull the trousers on over them | Socks, shoes, then underwear | Any order is just as quick
test: can i drink from your bottle | Better use your own, colds and cold sores spread through spit | Sure, sharing stops germs | Only if you wipe it on your shirt | Yes, water kills all germs
test: cant sleep, gonna watch videos on my tablet | Lie quietly, breathe slowly or read a calm book | Play an exciting game instead | Watch videos until you're tired | Turn all the lights on
test: nan says no swimming for an hour after lunch or ull drown, true? | No good evidence for that after a normal meal | Yes, you'll sink for sure | Yes, wait exactly an hour | Yes, only after pasta
test: apples from the shop, just eat em? | Wash them first, they may have dirt or chemicals | They're clean already, shops wash all their fruit | Peel them with your teeth | Only wash them if they look dirty
explain: A boy in Texas ate chicken from a picnic that had sat in the sun for hours; that night he was sick again and again and needed a doctor because he could not keep water down.
explain: A girl in England stayed up on her tablet till two in the morning before a school trip; next day she was so tired she fell asleep on the bus and missed most of the museum.
fact: Food such as meat, rice and dairy left warm for more than a couple of hours can grow germs that cause food poisoning, so leftovers go in the fridge once they have cooled.
fact: Children who do not sleep enough find it hard to think, get cross more easily and are more likely to have accidents.
test: chicken's been in the car all day, still ok to eat? | No, warm meat can give you food poisoning | Yes, if it still smells fine when you open the box | Yes, if you heat it up again | Yes, if you eat it quickly
test: What is a likely result of sleeping only four hours before a test? | You are tired and think less clearly | You get a better mark, because you are excited | You grow faster | Nothing happens at your age
test: sis says she'll share my drink, she's got a cold | Better not, colds pass from shared cups | Yes, sharing drinks actually helps stop colds | Yes, colds don't spread that way | Only if she drinks first
test: my nan swears a boy at her church went swimming right after his dinner and got a cramp and drowned. so u have to wait an hour?? | No good evidence for that; the real rule is swim where an adult watches | Yes, always wait a full hour after eating | Yes, but only after a big dinner | Yes, and don't drink water before either
test: did u hear?? half the class got food poisoning from rice someone left out overnight at the party, they were sick for days. real lesson? | Warm leftover food grows germs, so put it in the fridge | Rice is always dangerous to eat | Reheating fixes any food left out | Only meat can cause food poisoning
test: apparently that new kid's cold sores are cos he's dirty, everyone's saying not to sit near him. pass it on? | No, it's untrue and unkind; just don't share cups | Yes, so nobody in the class catches anything off him | Yes, if you only tell a couple of friends | Yes, cold sores only come from being dirty
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a person uses his own cup or bottle. | Colds, influenza and cold sores are spread through saliva | Cups belong exclusively to their owners by law in most countries | Shared cups render drinks less cold | Sharing a cup causes the drink to taste different
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the effect of screens used shortly before sleep? | Their light and excitement render sleep more difficult | They render sleep deeper and longer, as the eyes grow weary | They have no effect upon sleep whatsoever | They assist persons who cannot fall asleep
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning grapes given to small children. | They are cut lengthways, lest they block the airway | They are given whole, being soft enough to be swallowed safely | They are frozen in order to be safer | They are forbidden to all children
exam: Ascertain the condition of a bedroom most conducive to deep sleep. | Dark, quiet and cool | Bright, warm and with a television on | Noisy, so as to mask other sounds | Hot and with the window shut
